  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 325 AM EDT Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Crnt radar imagery shows a band of widespread light to \nmoderate rain impacting most of our cwa this morning associated with \ndeep moisture advection and channeled 700-500mb vorticity. This \nmoisture and energy should shift east of VT by 15z this morning with \nsome breaks in the overcast expected by early aftn. These breaks \nshould allow temps to warm into the mid 70s to lower 80s with dwpts \nin the 60s, creating sfc based CAPE values in the 800-1200 J/kg. \nHowever, sounding data does suggest a warm nose of 13-14C air near \n800mb, which could act as a cap until better forcing and cooler air \naloft arrives late in the day/this evening. Also, subsidence/dry air \nbehind initial moisture surge this morning may limit areal coverage \nof any convection this aftn. SPC continues to place most of our cwa \nin a marginal risk for severe, but feel threat is very conditions \nwith limited areal coverage and intensity of storms anticipated due \nto the combination of factors outlined above. However, if a \nconvective updraft can grow tall enough, given the developing \nmid/upper lvl lvl wind fields, an isolated gusty thunderstorm would \nbe possible. The threat for stronger storms dissipates after sunset, \nbut additional showers are likely overnight associated with our sfc \ncold frnt and potent 700-500mb s/w energy dropping southward. The \ndynamics and ribbon of moisture wl produce a line of quick moving \nshowers from west to east acrs our cwa btwn 03z-09z Monday. \n\nModerate caa develops behind the boundary for Monday with lingering \nclouds, especially in favorable upslope regions thru early aftn, \nbefore drier air develops and skies become mostly sunny. Highs only \nin the upper 50s to upper 60s with brisk northwest winds. Building \n1028mb high pres and associated deep dry layer wl result in mostly \nclear skies and light winds on Monday night, expect areas of fog \nwith some patchy frost likely in the dacks/nek by Tues morning. Lows \nfrom the upper 20s to mid/upper 40s anticipated acrs the region.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: The large scale synoptic pattern conts to indicate a \nfast/zonal flow acrs the northern CONUS for most of this upcoming \nweek. This will provide our cwa with changeable weather conditions \nevery couple of days but the probability of significant or \nwidespread hazardous weather impacts is very low attm. Temps wl \ntrend at or slightly below normal for most of this upcoming week \nwith highest probability of showers late Weds into Thurs. Precip \nsetup looks very similar to our crnt scenario with 700-500mb \nvorticity lobe angling acrs our cwa as sfc cold frnt is dropping \nsouthward. This wl support expanding areal coverage of showers and \nembedded heavier downpours late Weds into Thurs, especially with pw \nvalues near 1.5\". Similar to this mornings activity, highest \npops/qpf looks to be acrs central/southern areas, closer to the \ndeeper moisture/weak instability, but this could change as the week \nprogresses. Temps mostly in the upper 60s to mid 70s with lows mid \n40s to mid 50s.",
